<section class="inner-sec" style="padding-top: 10px;">
  <div class="proof-band">
    <div class="sec-eyebrow">Where the line is</div>
    <h2>It schedules. Your therapists treat.</h2>
    <p>Everything a physical therapy front desk does that isn't clinical — answering, booking, reminding, rescheduling, chasing the people who drifted off their plan — is work this can take. Everything touching a diagnosis, an exercise, an authorisation or a chart stays with your team, because a confident wrong answer in any of those is worse than a missed call.</p>
    <div class="proof-points">
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>No clinical advice, ever</strong>
        <span>It never suggests an exercise, comments on an injury, or tells someone whether therapy will help. Those go to a therapist, every time.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>It never verifies benefits or visit limits</strong>
        <span>It can share what you've published about plans and cash rates. What a specific policy authorises reaches a human, because a wrong answer there becomes a billing problem.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>It never opens a chart</strong>
        <span>Front-desk logistics only — a name, a number, a reason to follow up. No records, no history, which is why most clinics need no compliance layer at all.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ppoint">
        <strong>Adherence, not pressure</strong>
        <span>Reminders and easy rescheduling are how a plan gets finished. It nudges once and makes it simple to move a visit; it doesn't hound your patients.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

